{"baseRefName":"main","body":"## Why?\n\nA failed MCP call logged only tool names:\n\n```\nDEBUG MCP tool call callId=call_b5d4… tool=mcp\nDEBUG MCP tool result callId=call_b5d4… errorClass=unknown status=error tool=mcp\nERROR MCP tool call failed; check server availability and auth. Review not posted. additionalTools= errorClasses=[\"unknown\"] servers=[\"unknown\"] tools=[\"mcp\"]\n```\n\nThree questions went unanswered: which server was involved, which repository the agent asked about, and why the call failed. The third is the important one — the gateway rejects a malformed payload *itself*, so the repository never reaches the server and no server-side log can explain it. The class `unknown` was the only trace, and it said nothing.\n\nReproduced against the live DeepWiki endpoint: a payload nested inside `args` fails identically against an unreachable server, while a well-formed call reports the connection failure — so the nested payload never touched the network.\n\n## This PR will...\n\n- **Name the server for every gateway shape.** The `server` argument is read once, before the shape dispatch, and the shapes that were indistinguishable (`connect`, `describe:<tool>`, `instructions`, `list`) are named. A call that addresses a server without naming one names the only server there is; the adapter-local shapes (the status listing, an install) stay server-less, because they never reached a server.\n- **Report the harness rejection reason.** A failure with an empty details object is the harness's own error envelope — a thrown tool, a schema validation error, a blocked or aborted call — and its reason is now logged in the debug output and the thinking trace. Adapter and script-mode results carry their own fields, so server text is never read.\n- **Disclose the repositories a failed call named**, under a per-server declaration (`loggableArgs` in the MCP config). The declaration is the authorization: the values it covers are disclosed once they are shaped like an identifier, private repository names included. The pattern is a syntax filter, not validation — CI.md says so, and the operator owns the decision.\n\nThe same failure now reads:\n\n```\nDEBUG MCP tool result callId=call_b5d4… detail=Gateway params were nested inside `args`; pass them top-level (for example, mcp({ search: \"...\" }) or mcp({ tool: \"...\", args: {} })). errorClass=unknown status=error tool=mcp\nERROR MCP tool call failed; check server availability and auth. Review not posted. additionalTools= errorClasses=[\"unknown\"] repos=[\"esafak/pi-reviewer\"] servers=[\"unknown\"] tools=[\"mcp\"]\n```\n\n`servers=[\"unknown\"]` next to `repos=[…]` is deliberate: attribution (which server served the call) and authorization (whose declaration covers the disclosed value) are separate. That payload named no server, so none is claimed.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `vpr lint` clean; `vpr test` 1025 passed / 5 skipped (58 files).\n- New tests cover the harness rejection reason, the script-mode counterexample (whose content can hold server text), the per-shape identities, the disclosure policy and its bounds, and the ambiguous-server-name rule.\n- Checked against the live DeepWiki MCP endpoint: a real call's repository identifier is read; a private repository name is disclosed; a value that names no repository is disclosed only because the declaration authorizes it.","headRefName":"feature/mcp-failure-diagnostics","isDraft":false,"number":169,"state":"MERGED","title":"feat(review): name the MCP server, repository, and rejection reason","updatedAt":"2026-10-20T18:24:46Z","url":"https://github.com/esafak/pi-reviewer/pull/169"}
